 "faqs": {
  "heading": "Face to face training and CareStream, answered",
  "groups": [
   {
    "group": "About the training",
    "items": [
     {
      "q": "Does online training replace face to face training?",
      "a": "No. For topics such as moving and handling, a competent person must still show and observe the technique. CareStream sits alongside the session and shows what people retained."
     },
     {
      "q": "Where does the follow up training come from?",
      "a": "From your own policies. You pick the topic module, and CareStream builds the lessons and questions from the policy text, so it matches what your service does. You can edit and preview it before you assign it."
     },
     {
      "q": "What if a carer gets a question wrong?",
      "a": "It appears in their Follow-up tab. They can choose Learn &amp; retry, a short lesson on that point followed by a fresh question, or Just retry the same question, as often as they need. Treat it as information, not failure."
     },
     {
      "q": "How soon after the session should the follow up go out?",
      "a": "Within a few days is a sensible rule: early enough that the session is fresh, late enough to show what stuck. You choose when to allocate it, and you can set a due date."
     },
     {
      "q": "Face to face vs online training: which is better?",
      "a": "Neither replaces the other. A room is good for discussion and hands on demonstration, and a competent person must still show and observe practical skills. Online follow up is good for checking understanding and closing gaps. Used together, this is blended learning."
     },
     {
      "q": "Is face to face safeguarding training enough on its own?",
      "a": "A good session is a strong start, but safeguarding training only protects people if staff remember it when it matters. A short follow up built from your own safeguarding policy shows who understood and who needs another look. The <a href=\"/staff-training/safeguarding-adults-and-children\">safeguarding course</a> covers the topic itself."
     },
     {
      "q": "How to measure training effectiveness in a care home",
      "a": "Look at four things: whether people liked the training, whether they learned it, whether they act differently on the floor, and whether outcomes improve. A knowledge check after the session covers learning, observation covers behaviour and <a href=\"/care-audits\">audits</a> show results. CareStream covers the knowledge check and the follow up."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "group": "About inspections",
    "items": [
     {
      "q": "Will an inspector accept this as evidence?",
      "a": "The Care Quality Commission looks for staff who are competent in their role, not only trained. A record of questions, lessons and results helps show that. See <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2014/2936/regulation/18\">Regulation 18 on staffing</a> for the wording."
     },
     {
      "q": "What are the CQC training requirements for care home staff?",
      "a": "Regulation 18 says staff must receive the support, training, professional development, supervision and appraisal they need to carry out their duties. It does not list courses, so each provider decides what its staff need and must be able to show it. The <a href=\"https://www.cqc.org.uk/guidance-regulation/providers/regulations-service-providers-and-managers/health-social-care-act/regulation-18\">CQC guidance on Regulation 18</a> explains how inspectors look at it."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
