 "guide": {
  "heading": "Keeping supervision records you can stand behind",
  "lede": "A good record is short, dated and specific. It does not need to be long. It needs to show that the conversation happened and what each of you agreed to do.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "h3": "Before the session",
    "paragraphs": [
     "Look at what the person has been asked to do since last time, and at how they got on. A few minutes of preparation changes the conversation, because you arrive with questions and not guesses.",
     "Take the open actions from the last record and ask about each one first. An action that is raised again every time tells you something is stuck."
    ],
    "checklist": [
     "Open the actions agreed last time",
     "Check training that is overdue or due soon",
     "Note any low scores worth asking about",
     "Decide one thing to praise and one thing to explore"
    ]
   },
   {
    "h3": "Writing the record",
    "paragraphs": [
     "Write it in plain words soon after, while it is fresh. Record what the person said in their own terms where you can, and keep your feedback separate so it is clear whose view is whose.",
     "Keep each agreed action to one clear task, with an owner and, where it helps, a date. A vague action such as <em>improve confidence</em> cannot be closed. <em>Shadow a colleague at two mealtimes</em> can."
